79
01:13:59.000 --> 01:15:00.000
Case file acknowledges Jay Hart's right to the presumption of innocence. Colleen, Evelyn and Clinton's families are still fighting for a retrial. The victims' loved ones have no doubt that the case would have been treated very differently if they weren't Indigenous. From the start, police dismissed their concerns due to the prejudices about their community. Michelle Jarrett, the aunt of Evelyn Greenup, has said that if Colleen's disappearance was taken seriously, then Evelyn and Clinton might still be alive today. When two of the cases later went to trial, Indigenous witnesses found themselves up against a legal system that was foreign to them. The juries selected didn't include any Indigenous people and they weren't educated on Indigenous communication styles,
案卷承认杰伊·哈特享有无罪推定权。科琳、伊芙琳和克林顿的家属仍在为重新审判而战。受害者的亲人们毫不怀疑，如果他们不是土著人，这个案件的处理方式会大不相同。从一开始，由于对他们社区的偏见，警察就对他们的担忧置之不理。伊芙琳·格林纳普的姑姑米歇尔·杰瑞特曾说过，如果科琳的失踪被认真对待，那么伊芙琳和克林顿今天可能还活着。后来其中两个案件进入审判程序时，土著证人发现自己面对的是一个对他们来说陌生的法律系统。挑选的陪审团不包含任何土著人，也没有对他们进行有关土著沟通方式的教育，

80
01:15:00.000 --> 01:15:51.000
which possibly led to witnesses being misinterpreted as untrustworthy. For example, some Indigenous witnesses would say yes as a polite response to being asked a question before going on to give evidence that contradicted what jurors heard as their previous answer. In 2016, the New South Wales Police Commissioner Andrew Scipione met with all three families to formally acknowledge that institutional racism had hindered the initial handling of the case. He stated, I want to publicly acknowledge that the New South Wales Police Force could have done more for your families when these crimes first occurred and how this added to your pain as a grieving community.
这可能导致证人被误解为不可信。例如，一些土著证人在被问及问题时会礼貌地回答“是”，然后继续提供与陪审团听到他们之前的回答相矛盾的证据。2016年，新南威尔士州警察局长安德鲁·西皮奥内会见了所有三个家庭，正式承认机构性种族主义阻碍了案件的初期处理。他表示：“我想公开承认，当这些罪行首次发生时，新南威尔士州警察部队本可以为你们的家属做更多的事情，以及这如何加剧了你们作为哀痛社区的痛苦。”

81
01:15:51.000 --> 01:16:33.000
And for that, I am sorry. Colleen Craig's family have never stopped looking for her body. In 2011, they thought they might have found her when some bones were discovered not far from Bowerville, but these turned out to be animal remains. To this day, Colleen's siblings, cousins, nephews and nieces still search for her. The fact that she has never been found has contributed to their grief. As one of Colleen's cousins told a parliamentary committee, it is something that is missing within all of us.
“为此，我深感抱歉。”科琳·克雷格的家属从未停止寻找她的尸体。2011年，当在鲍尔维尔附近发现一些骨头时，他们以为可能找到了她，但这些骨头被证明是动物遗骸。直到今天，科琳的兄弟姐妹、表亲、侄女和外甥女仍在寻找她。她一直没有被找到的事实加剧了他们的悲痛。正如科琳的一位表亲告诉议会委员会的那样，这是我们所有人心中所缺失的一部分。

82
01:16:33.000 --> 01:17:32.000
Not actually knowing where she is and what has happened to her is something that we carry every single day. Those closest to Evelyn Greenup have battled guilt as well as sorrow. Her grandmother Patricia has suffered as she heard Evelyn cry out on the night in question but was unable to reach her. Evelyn's mother Rebecca has also been tormented by the fact that Evelyn went missing from the same room where she was sleeping. In the years after her daughter's death, Rebecca directed her pain inwards and struggled with alcoholism. In her own words, My heart was ripped apart and I felt numb as I knew Evelyn was never coming back to me. I would never be able to give her a hug or give her a kiss, tell her everything is alright,
不知道她在哪里、她发生了什么，是我们每天都在承受的事情。伊芙琳·格林纳普最亲近的人除了悲伤之外，还在与内疚作斗争。她的祖母帕特里夏在事发当晚听到伊芙琳哭喊却无法接触到她，为此饱受折磨。伊芙琳的母亲丽贝卡也受到伊芙琳在自己睡觉的同一个房间里失踪这一事实的折磨。在她女儿死后的几年里，丽贝卡将她的痛苦转向内收，并与酒精中毒作斗争。用她自己的话说：“我的心碎了，我感到麻木，因为我知道伊芙琳永远不会回到我身边了。我再也不能给她一个拥抱或一个吻，告诉她一切都会好起来的，”

83
01:17:32.000 --> 01:18:17.000
and I would never see my daughter grow up into a beautiful woman. I was always treated like I had no right to Evelyn's information on updates of the investigation and I was always left out of everything because everyone blamed me. No one will ever know how I feel because I was Evelyn's mother and had that special bond with her. I carried her for nine months. I felt her move and grow in my womb and I gave birth to my first born child, Evelyn Clarice Greenup. Even though Clinton's speedy duo was the first victim found,
“我也永远不会看到我的女儿长成一个美丽的女人。我一直被当作我对伊芙琳关于调查更新的信息没有权利，我总是被排挤在一切之外，因为每个人都责怪我。没有人会知道我的感受，因为我是伊芙琳的母亲，与她有着那种特殊的联系。我怀了她九个月。我感到她在我的子宫里移动和生长，我生下了我的第一个孩子，伊芙琳·克拉丽丝·格林纳普。”尽管克林顿·斯皮迪·杜罗是被发现的第一个受害者，

84
01:18:17.000 --> 01:19:10.000
it later emerged that the police had not done a thorough job in bringing him back to his family. A year after Clinton's murder, two bones belonging to him were found in the bush. His family learnt of this oversight 12 years after they lost Clinton when his autopsy report was accidentally shared with them. This was an extremely painful discovery, especially as the burial of human remains is a very sensitive matter within indigenous culture. Clinton's family members still struggled to speak of the boy they lost, due in part to the sense of injustice they feel from the trial's outcome in 1994. His brother Troy has said he had difficulty even speaking to his wife about Clinton
后来发现，警方在将他送回家人身边这件事上并没有做好彻底的工作。在克林顿遇害一年后，人们在丛林中发现了属于他的两根骨头。在他去世12年后，当他的尸检报告被不小心分享给他们时，他的家人才得知这一疏忽。这是一个极其痛苦的发现，特别是在原住民文化中，安葬人类遗体是一件非常敏感的事情。由于1994年审判结果带来的不公感，克林顿的家人们至今仍很难谈论这个失去的男孩。他的弟弟特洛伊曾说，他甚至很难和妻子谈论克林顿

85
01:19:10.000 --> 01:19:55.000
and has never really learned how to deal with the pain and loss of trust in the justice system. The constant battle with the police, the legal system and the government over the past three decades has left all of Clinton's loved ones feeling despondent. The younger generation has picked up the fight from their parents, but the last thing they want is for future generations to have to do the same thing. In Clinton's father's words, Each time we think we are going to get justice, we are let down. We keep going, but it is hard.
并且从未真正学会如何应对痛苦以及对司法系统失去的信任。在过去的三十家里，与警方、司法系统以及政府的不断抗争，让克林顿所有的亲人都感到沮丧。年轻一代从父母手中接过了这场抗争，但他们最不希望看到的就是后代也不得不这样做。用克林顿父亲的话说，每次我们以为能得到公正时，都会失望。我们一直在坚持，但这很难。

86
01:19:55.000 --> 01:20:41.000
Those who knew Colleen Craig described her as vibrant, kind and well-liked. She had a tender and loving nature, as demonstrated by her care for younger children. Her family said she had a strong character with a clear moral compass, while still being something of a prankster who liked to make jokes. At the time that she disappeared, Colleen had completed a certificate and some work experience in preparation for achieving her dream of becoming a preschool teacher. Before she left her hometown of Sortel to go to Barreville in the days before her death, she kissed her mother goodbye.
认识科琳·克雷格的人都形容她充满活力、善良且受人喜爱。她天性温柔慈爱，她对年幼孩子的照顾就是明证。她的家人说她性格坚强，有着清晰的道德底线，同时又有点喜欢恶作剧和开玩笑。在失踪前，科琳已经完成了一个证书课程并积累了一些工作经验，为实现她成为一名幼儿园老师的梦想做准备。在她离开家乡索特尔前往巴雷维尔的前几天，她吻别了母亲。

87
